==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*leudaną}}, from {{der|ang|ine-pro|*lewdʰ-||to grow, come up}}. Cognates include Old Saxon {{m|osx|liodan}}, Old High German {{m|goh|liotan}}, Gothic {{m|got|𐌻𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{desc|fa|رستن|tr=rostan}}, Albanian {{m|sq|lind||to be born, to spring}} < {{m|sq|lej||to generate, produce, give birth to}}, Ancient Greek {{m|grc|ἐλεύθερος||free, freeman}}, Latin {{m|la|liber||free}}, Old Irish {{m|sga|luss||plant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈleːodɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|head=lēodan}}

# to [[grow]]
#: ''Of ðam twige '''ludon''' reðe wæstme'': from that branch sprang dire fruits. (Cædmon's ''Metrical Paraphrase'')

